IMPORT prelude
IMPORT `coq-common`
IMPORT `coq-access`

-- Coroners Act 2003 (Queensland) -- worked cases for coq-access.l4 (ss 52-57, 59-62A).
--
-- Every assertion is a claim about the ENCODING on stated facts, not about the law. Expected values were written by hand
-- from the deposited text (act-2003-013, in force from 2026-10-29), never copied from the encoding's output. No scenario
-- life raised this Act; the facts are the encoder's, HYPOTHETICAL throughout. Every "considers" and "is satisfied" of
-- a coroner is given as a fact.
--
-- Unofficial version. Based on content from the Queensland Legislation website at 6 November 2026. (c) The State of
-- Queensland, CC BY 4.0. See SOURCE-LICENSE.md.
-- BASELINE ENCODING. Not for public use as advice. See NOTES.md.

§ `ss 52, 54-57 -- investigation documents`

-- Argument order: document, part wanted, s 52 bar, confidential information, sufficient interest, public interest and
-- family consulted, refused in the public interest, consented.
-- A widow asks for the autopsy report's cause of death; the coroner accepts her sufficient interest.
#ASSERT `Can I see the investigation documents question` `a coronial document (prepared for the investigation, such as the autopsy report or the police report to the coroner)` `information on who died, how, when, where, or what caused the death (the s 45(2) matters)` `nothing s 52(1)(a), (b), (d) or (e) lists` FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `yes, if the coroner consents: the coroner may consent, on conditions the coroner considers necessary (ss 54(2), (3), 55)`
#ASSERT `Can I see the investigation documents question` `a coronial document (prepared for the investigation, such as the autopsy report or the police report to the coroner)` `information on who died, how, when, where, or what caused the death (the s 45(2) matters)` `nothing s 52(1)(a), (b), (d) or (e) lists` FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE TRUE EQUALS `yes: the coroner has consented; you must comply with any conditions, unless you have a reasonable excuse (maximum penalty 100 penalty units or 2 years imprisonment) (s 55)`
-- The deceased's mental health history in the same report, not relevant to the s 45(2) matters.
#ASSERT `Can I see the investigation documents question` `a coronial document (prepared for the investigation, such as the autopsy report or the police report to the coroner)` `other information about someone's personal affairs, such as health` `nothing s 52(1)(a), (b), (d) or (e) lists` FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `no: the coroner must not give you access to this part of the document (s 52(1))`
-- Hospital records the coroner obtained, the same kind of information: the request is passed on (s 57).
#ASSERT `Can I see the investigation documents question` `another investigation document (prepared by someone else, such as a police document or hospital records)` `other information about someone's personal affairs, such as health` `nothing s 52(1)(a), (b), (d) or (e) lists` FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `no: the coroner must not give you access to this part of the document (s 52(1)); as the coroner did not prepare it, the coroner must pass your request to the entity that prepared it and tell you in writing (s 57)`
-- The result of the test the applicant asked for under s 23A.
#ASSERT `Can I see the investigation documents question` `a coronial document (prepared for the investigation, such as the autopsy report or the police report to the coroner)` `the result of a test I applied for under s 23A` `nothing s 52(1)(a), (b), (d) or (e) lists` FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `yes, if the coroner consents: the coroner may consent, on conditions the coroner considers necessary (ss 54(2), (3), 55)`
#ASSERT `Can I see the investigation documents question` `a coronial document (prepared for the investigation, such as the autopsy report or the police report to the coroner)` `information not about anyone's personal affairs` `legal professional privilege (s 52(1)(a))` FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `no: the coroner must not give you access to this part of the document (s 52(1))`
-- A safety inspector's interview record compelled under workplace safety laws.
#ASSERT `Can I see the investigation documents question` `another investigation document (prepared by someone else, such as a police document or hospital records)` `information not about anyone's personal affairs` `information compelled from someone under another Act (s 52(1)(d))` FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `no: the coroner must not give you access to this part of the document (s 52(1)); as the coroner did not prepare it, the coroner must pass your request to the entity that prepared it and tell you in writing (s 57)`
#ASSERT `Can I see the investigation documents question` `a coronial document (prepared for the investigation, such as the autopsy report or the police report to the coroner)` `information not about anyone's personal affairs` `information likely to prejudice a fair trial, an investigation, a confidential source, someone's safety, law enforcement or an escape from custody (s 52(1)(b))` FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `no: the coroner must not give you access to this part of the document (s 52(1))`
-- A journalist with no sufficient interest; the coroner sees no public interest.
#ASSERT `Can I see the investigation documents question` `a coronial document (prepared for the investigation, such as the autopsy report or the police report to the coroner)` `information on who died, how, when, where, or what caused the death (the s 45(2) matters)` `nothing s 52(1)(a), (b), (d) or (e) lists` FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `no: the coroner may consent only if satisfied you have a sufficient interest, or if the access is in the public interest and a family member has been consulted as far as practicable (s 54(3))`
-- Consent given without either ground is still not a ground: the answer follows s 54(3).
#ASSERT `Can I see the investigation documents question` `a coronial document (prepared for the investigation, such as the autopsy report or the police report to the coroner)` `information on who died, how, when, where, or what caused the death (the s 45(2) matters)` `nothing s 52(1)(a), (b), (d) or (e) lists` FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE TRUE EQUALS `no: the coroner may consent only if satisfied you have a sufficient interest, or if the access is in the public interest and a family member has been consulted as far as practicable (s 54(3))`
#ASSERT `Can I see the investigation documents question` `a coronial document (prepared for the investigation, such as the autopsy report or the police report to the coroner)` `information on who died, how, when, where, or what caused the death (the s 45(2) matters)` `nothing s 52(1)(a), (b), (d) or (e) lists` FALSE FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `yes, if the coroner consents: the coroner may consent, on conditions the coroner considers necessary (ss 54(2), (3), 55)`
-- A statement with unsubstantiated allegations: refused in the public interest although the family is eligible.
#ASSERT `Can I see the investigation documents question` `a coronial document (prepared for the investigation, such as the autopsy report or the police report to the coroner)` `information on who died, how, when, where, or what caused the death (the s 45(2) matters)` `nothing s 52(1)(a), (b), (d) or (e) lists` FALSE TRUE FALSE TRUE FALSE EQUALS `no: the coroner has refused access in the public interest (s 56(1)); you may apply to the coroner to amend or revoke the refusal (s 56(4))`
#ASSERT `Can I see the investigation documents question` `another investigation document (prepared by someone else, such as a police document or hospital records)` `information on who died, how, when, where, or what caused the death (the s 45(2) matters)` `nothing s 52(1)(a), (b), (d) or (e) lists` TRUE TRUE FALSE FALSE TRUE EQUALS `not under s 54: confidential information the coroner obtained under s 17 (or the Child Protection Act 1999 s 159P or the Public Health Act 2005 ss 56, 86) may be accessed only under those provisions (s 54(7))`

§ `ss 59-62 -- property held as evidence`

-- Argument order: investigation document not seized by police, owner, no longer required, lawful to possess,
-- guidelines make return undesirable, could not be returned.
#ASSERT `Will my property be returned question` TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `division 5 does not apply: an investigation document not seized by a police officer is dealt with under division 4 (s 59(2))`
#ASSERT `Will my property be returned question` FALSE FALSE TRUE TRUE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `s 60 does not apply: you are not its owner (the person who appears to the coroner to be its lawful owner, or the personal representative of the person who died)`
-- The executor asks for the deceased's phone and wallet, no longer needed.
#ASSERT `Will my property be returned question` FALSE TRUE TRUE TRUE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `yes: the coroner must order it returned to you as soon as the coroner decides it is no longer required (s 60(1))`
#ASSERT `Will my property be returned question` FALSE TRUE FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `not yet: it is still required; until it is returned the coroner must let you inspect it, and copy it if it is a document, unless that is impracticable or unreasonable (s 62)`
-- An unlicensed firearm; a cracked safety helmet.
#ASSERT `Will my property be returned question` FALSE TRUE TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `no: it is not lawful for you to possess it, so it must not be returned and becomes State property (ss 60(2)(a), 61(1)(b))`
#ASSERT `Will my property be returned question` FALSE TRUE TRUE TRUE TRUE FALSE EQUALS `no: under the State Coroner's guidelines it is not desirable to return it because of its nature, condition and value, so it becomes State property (ss 60(2)(b), 61(1)(b))`
#ASSERT `Will my property be returned question` FALSE TRUE TRUE TRUE FALSE TRUE EQUALS `no: the coroner could not return it to you after reasonable efforts, so it has become State property (s 61(1)(a))`

§ `s 62A -- an inquest exhibit`

-- Argument order: owner, sufficient interest, public interest and family consulted, consented.
#ASSERT `Can I access the inquest exhibit question` TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `s 62A does not apply to the owner: the owner may inspect it under s 62`
-- The family's engineer wants to test the failed harness tendered at the inquest.
#ASSERT `Can I access the inquest exhibit question` FALSE TRUE FALSE TRUE EQUALS `yes: the coroner has consented to your access (s 62A(2)(a))`
#ASSERT `Can I access the inquest exhibit question` FALSE TRUE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `possibly: the coroner may consent to your access (s 62A(3))`
#ASSERT `Can I access the inquest exhibit question` FALSE FALSE TRUE FALSE EQUALS `possibly: the coroner may consent to your access (s 62A(3))`
#ASSERT `Can I access the inquest exhibit question` FALSE FALSE FALSE FALSE EQUALS `no: you may access the exhibit only with the coroner's consent, and the coroner may consent only if satisfied you have a sufficient interest, or if the access is in the public interest and a family member has been consulted (s 62A(2), (3))`
#ASSERT `Can I access the inquest exhibit question` FALSE FALSE FALSE TRUE EQUALS `no: you may access the exhibit only with the coroner's consent, and the coroner may consent only if satisfied you have a sufficient interest, or if the access is in the public interest and a family member has been consulted (s 62A(2), (3))`
